// The proof of `number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant` — the contract claim:
// either the precondition fails, or the answer satisfies the postcondition.
//
// It is the corpus's one path-condition claim, and what kept it open is that
// its then arm is reached only when `is_tag` held — a fact that is a
// *disjunction* (`v = t1` or `v = t2`), and no rule window can spend one.
// What spends it is a case split, and a case split is a chain of cuts: each
// waypoint below is one state of the analysis written out as a term, and the
// engine closes the step between two of them.
//
//   the body            `copy(1) ; is_tag ; branch { number ; is_numbered } { … }`,
//                       opened by label so the contract's two halves stay
//                       calls — a waypoint can name them instead of spelling
//                       them out, and every goal below is that much smaller
//   the frame off       the precondition's body, unframed
//   the case tree       the outer branch, and the copy it fed on, moved into
//                       `is_tag`'s own arms
//   the value written   each arm holding the literal it tested equal to
//   the answers         each leaf run on that literal: `push true`, three times
//   the claim           `drop(1) ; push true`
//
// The middle cuts are the ones that spend something; the rest is the engine
// folding what a literal reaches, cut where a fold needs more than one hop.
//
// The waypoints are terms, in the language a residual prints in — so what the
// report says when a hop fails is what the next waypoint is written from, and
// the padding that a Hana sentence leaves implicit (`id(2) * push t1`) is here
// on the page where the engine can be argued with about it.

proof types_test::number_does_pre_and_post_is_constant =
    // Open the function under test, and the precondition it asks — and
    // nothing else. `number` and `is_numbered` stay calls, so every waypoint
    // below names them instead of spelling them out.
    inline(types_test::number_does_pre_and_post)
    inline(types_test::is_tag)

    // The precondition, unframed. Opening a call puts its whole body inside
    // the frame the call sat in — `id(1) * (copy(1) ; …)` — and every law
    // about a branch is stated about one that is not framed, so the first cut
    // is the same body with the padding spread across its factors.
    via {
        copy(1) ; id(1) * copy(1) ; id(3) * push types_test::t1 ;
        id(2) * equal ;
        id(1) *
        branch {
            drop(1) ; push true
        } {
            id(1) * push types_test::t2 ; equal
        } ;
        branch {
            call types_test::number ; call types_test::is_numbered
        } {
            drop(1) ; push true
        }
    } (right:

    // The case tree. The outer branch distributes into the arms of the test:
    // the arm that answered `true` decides it, and the arm that did not
    // carries the second test with it. The copy made up front is the copy
    // each arm makes for itself, so every branch here reads a value one deep
    // — which is the shape every law about a tested value is stated in.
    via {
        copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t1 ; id(1) * equal ;
        branch {
            call types_test::number ; call types_test::is_numbered
        } {
            copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t2 ; id(1) * equal ;
            branch {
                call types_test::number ; call types_test::is_numbered
            } {
                drop(1) ; push true
            }
        }
    } (right:

    // The path condition, spent: a value that tested equal to a literal *is*
    // that literal, inside the arm that saw `true`. Each arm now runs the
    // contract on a constant, which is what the disjunction was hiding.
    via {
        copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t1 ; id(1) * equal ;
        branch {
            drop(1) ; push types_test::t1 ; call types_test::number ;
            call types_test::is_numbered
        } {
            copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t2 ; id(1) * equal ;
            branch {
                drop(1) ; push types_test::t2 ; call types_test::number ;
                call types_test::is_numbered
            } {
                drop(1) ; push true
            }
        }
    } (right:

    // Every leaf answers `true`, and a tree of three `true`s is the claim.
    via {
        copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t1 ; id(1) * equal ;
        branch {
            drop(1) ; push true
        } {
            copy(1) ; id(2) * push types_test::t2 ; id(1) * equal ;
            branch { drop(1) ; push true } { drop(1) ; push true }
        }
    } (
        // Per leaf: past the shared test, into the arm, past the shared drop,
        // and run the contract on the literal the arm holds. The last arm is
        // `drop(1) ; push true` on both sides already, so `descend` is given
        // nothing for it and checks that claim rather than assuming it.
        left: peel descend(
            // `t1` is `number`'s first case: one saturation reaches it.
            then: peel inline egraph,
            else: peel descend(
                // `t2` is not, and neither is it `is_numbered`'s first case,
                // so the answers the leaf passes through are named one at a
                // time — and each cut opens only the half of the contract that
                // has to fold, which is what the labels are for.
                then: peel
                    // What `number` does with a `t2`: its first test is false,
                    // so the else arm. Only `number` opens — the postcondition
                    // is the same call on both sides of this cut.
                    via {
                        push types_test::t2 ; copy(1) ;
                        id(2) * push types_test::t2 ; id(1) * equal ;
                        branch {
                            drop(1) ; push types_test::t2 ; id(1) * push 2 ; tuple 2
                        } {
                            drop(1) ; tuple 0
                        } ;
                        call types_test::is_numbered
                    } (left: inline(types_test::number) egraph, right:
                    // …and the pair that arm builds. Nothing opens here: it is
                    // the engine folding a literal.
                    via {
                        push (types_test::t2, 2) ; call types_test::is_numbered
                    } (right:
                    // Then the same again for the postcondition: `(t2, 2)` is
                    // not the pair it tests for first either.
                    via {
                        push (types_test::t2, 2) ; id(1) * push types_test::t2 ;
                        id(2) * push 2 ; id(1) * tuple 2 ; equal
                    } (left: inline(types_test::is_numbered) egraph))))),
        right: egraph))));
